hange comes in pairs within a row (`0->c` at one x, `c->0` one cell away), which is exactly what a whole-row shift by one cell looks like.\n\n## Which rows move, and which way\n\nReading the rows of the object from the bottom upwards:\n\n- the bottom row of the object is unchanged,\n- the next row up is shifted one cell **left**,\n- the next is unchanged,\n- the next is shifted one cell **right**,\n- and this 4-row cycle (stay, left, stay, right) repeats all the way up.\n\nSo with `y1` = bottom row of the object, row `y` moves by `{1: -1, 3: +1}.get((y1 - y) % 4, 0)` cells. This is anchored at the bottom, not the top: in pair 1 the top row (distance 9, i.e. 9 % 4 = 1) shifts left, in pair 3 the top row (distance 11, 11 % 4 = 3) shifts right, and in pair 4 the top row (distance 6, 6 % 4 = 2) stays. The inspect run applied exactly this rule to every train input and reproduced all four outputs cell by cell.\n\n## Applying it to the test input\n\nThe colour-7 lattice spans rows 1..13, so row 13 is the fixed bottom. Going upward: row 12 shifts left, row 11 stays, row 10 shifts right, row 9 stays, row 8 shifts left, 7 stays, 6 shifts right, 5 stays, 4 shifts left, 3 stays, 2 shifts right, and row 1 (the top frame, distance 12) stays. The frame's left/right edges are never at the grid border, so shifting never pushes colour off the grid.\n\nThe program finds the background and object rows from the grid itself (no fixed colours or positions), then shifts each row with zero fill, emitting a canvas after each row that actually moves.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    rows = np.where((g != bg).any(axis=1))[0]\n    y0, y1 = rows.min(), rows.max()\n    out = g.copy()\n    W = g.shape[1]\n    for y in range(y1, y0 - 1, -1):\n        d = (y1 - y) % 4\n        s = {1: -1, 3: 1}.get(d, 0)\n        if s == 0:\n            continue\n        row = g[y]\n        new = np.full(W, bg, dtype=g.dtype)\n        if s > 0:\n            new[s:] = row[:W - s]\n        else:\n            new[:W + s] = row[-s:]\n        out[y] = new\n        direction = \"right\" if s > 0 else \"left\"\n        emit(out, f\"Row {y} is {y1 - y} rows above the bottom of the lattice; distance mod 4 = {d}, so shift it one cell {direction}.\")\n    emit(out, \"All rows processed: bottom-anchored cycle stay/left/stay/right applied; final result.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"es in pairs within a row (`0->c` at one x, `c->0` one cell away), which is exactly what a whole-row shift by one cell looks like."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the next row up is shifted one cell **left**,"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- the next is shifted one cell **right**,"}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output has exactly the same number of coloured cells and the same colour; only the horizontal position of individual rows changes."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"aa29e4be7140de5cb26c52e1c89cc7c439588294be675fa0f407f2bf8d836b12","task_sha256":"32002ed6957150260dc4ab70b4a7b2fd3fb8703fa8ce2baab22eb7446f373d4c","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
